## 1. Objective and scope

Design a prospective empirical study to:

- Quantify persistent tensile-strength differences among nominally identical 3D-printed polymer coupons.
- Determine which measurable manufacturing, material, environmental, geometric, and testing factors explain those differences.
- Separate systematic effects from random coupon-to-coupon variation, batch effects, machine effects, and measurement error.
- Distinguish competing mechanisms, including:
  - feedstock moisture or lot variation;
  - thermal-history and interlayer-bonding differences;
  - extrusion-flow or dimensional variation;
  - orientation, raster, and toolpath effects;
  - machine, build-plate location, and print-time drift;
  - porosity or other internal defects;
  - conditioning and test-environment effects;
  - grip alignment, strain measurement, or test-frame effects.

Primary outcome: tensile strength at maximum force, calculated using a prespecified cross-sectional-area definition.

Secondary outcomes: elastic modulus, yield stress where applicable, strain at break, failure location and mode, density/void indicators, dimensional variation, and force–displacement/strain-curve features.

The study is limited initially to one polymer formulation, coupon geometry, nominal slicing profile, and printer technology. Conclusions should not automatically be generalized to other polymers, geometries, printers, or process settings.

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means coupons share the same CAD geometry, material specification, nominal process settings, and test procedure; it does not imply identical realized dimensions, thermal histories, or microstructures.
- Tensile strength is affected by hierarchical sources of variation: material lot, drying batch, printer, build, plate position, coupon, conditioning batch, operator, and test session.
- Coupon results within the same build are correlated and must not be treated as independent replicates.
- Some candidate causes may be correlated—for example, nozzle temperature, chamber temperature, print order, and plate position—so deliberate randomization or controlled perturbation is needed for causal attribution.
- Destructive testing prevents repeat tensile measurements on the same coupon. Measurement repeatability must therefore be assessed through calibration artifacts, duplicate dimensional or imaging measurements, and comparable matched coupons.
- All protocol changes, exclusions, transformations, and outcome definitions will be specified before examining outcome associations.
- The study will use the same applicable tensile-test standard and specimen geometry throughout. Deviations will be documented rather than silently corrected.
- Production metadata will be recorded at the highest feasible temporal resolution and retained in raw form.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Measurement-system qualification

Before comparing manufacturing causes:

1. Calibrate and verify the load cell, extensometer or optical strain system, displacement channel, environmental sensors, balances, dimensional instruments, and printer temperature sensors.
2. Quantify:
   - load and strain accuracy;
   - dimensional measurement repeatability and reproducibility;
   - grip alignment and bending;
   - operator and test-session effects;
   - sensitivity to crosshead speed, preload, and gripping procedure within allowable tolerances.
3. Use alignment specimens or strain gauges on opposite specimen faces to estimate bending strain.
4. Conduct a blinded gauge repeatability and reproducibility study for dimensional measurements, defect classification, and fracture-mode coding.
5. Establish acceptance limits. Do not proceed if measurement uncertainty is large enough to obscure the smallest strength difference considered practically important.

### Phase B: Baseline variance decomposition

Produce a balanced, replicated baseline set under one locked nominal recipe.

Recommended hierarchy:

- At least two material lots, if lot variation is within scope.
- At least three printers of the same model, or repeated machine periods if only one printer is available.
- Multiple independent builds per printer and lot.
- Multiple coupons per build, distributed across predefined plate positions.
- Repeated test sessions, with test order randomized across printer, build, and plate position.

Design features:

- Randomly assign unique coupon identifiers before printing.
- Randomize or rotate plate layouts so geometry and plate location are not confounded.
- Randomize tensile-test order after conditioning.
- Blind test operators and image/defect assessors to printer, lot, and process metadata where feasible.
- Include duplicate builds on different days.
- Record failed prints and invalid tests; do not analyze only successful coupons.
- Use a preregistered hierarchical model to estimate variance attributable to lot, printer, build, position, conditioning, test session, and residual coupon variation.

This phase establishes whether differences are primarily between machines, between builds, spatially patterned within builds, drifting over print time, or residual at the coupon level.

### Phase C: Controlled multifactor experiment

Use a randomized blocked factorial or response-surface design to perturb plausible causal factors across realistic operating ranges. Select ranges that remain process-valid and safe.

Candidate controlled factors:

- feedstock moisture state or drying protocol;
- nozzle temperature;
- bed and chamber or ambient temperature;
- print speed and volumetric flow rate;
- extrusion multiplier;
- layer height;
- raster angle and specimen orientation;
- cooling-fan setting;
- time since nozzle installation or controlled nozzle condition;
- conditioning temperature, humidity, and duration;
- test speed, if uncertainty remains after measurement qualification.

Blocking factors:

- material lot;
- printer;
- day;
- build;
- plate;
- test session.

Include:

- center-point replicates to measure pure error and detect nonlinearity;
- repeated baseline settings in every block to detect drift;
- deliberate randomization of factor settings, constrained only where changing settings mid-build would introduce carryover;
- washout or stabilization periods following temperature or material changes;
- sufficient replication for principal two-factor interactions, especially moisture × temperature, temperature × speed, flow × speed, orientation × raster, and conditioning × material lot.

Do not change many process factors together using only “good” versus “bad” recipes; that would not identify which factor caused a difference.

### Phase D: Mechanism-discrimination study

From Phase C, select matched groups with:

1. similar tensile strength but different process histories;
2. different tensile strength despite similar external dimensions;
3. high and low strength within the same build;
4. failure in the gauge section versus near grips or transitions.

Apply enhanced characterization to a prespecified stratified sample. This links strength differences to proposed mechanisms rather than relying only on predictive correlations.

### Competing explanations and distinguishing observations

| Explanation | Expected observations | Evidence against |
|---|---|---|
| Feedstock moisture or degradation | Moisture predicts strength after blocking by lot; voids, surface defects, or altered fracture behavior increase; controlled drying changes outcomes | Drying changes measured moisture but not strength or internal defects |
| Inadequate interlayer bonding | Strength tracks realized thermal history; fracture follows layer or raster interfaces; microscopy shows poor coalescence; higher bonding temperature improves strength within range | Fracture is predominantly through roads, and thermal history has no conditional association |
| Under-/over-extrusion or geometry | Strength changes with realized cross-section, mass per unit length, bead dimensions, or density; area correction materially alters estimates | Groups remain different after precise geometry and density adjustment |
| Internal porosity | Lower density or higher imaged void fraction predicts lower strength and initiates fracture | Void metrics are similar across strength groups or do not localize near initiation sites |
| Machine/build/plate thermal heterogeneity | Spatial gradients repeat across builds; local plate/chamber temperatures mediate position effects | Position effects disappear or fail to replicate despite precise location tracking |
| Material-lot chemistry or aging | Differences persist across printers and settings and follow lot; thermal or spectroscopic proxies differ if available | Lot effect vanishes under common drying and processing |
| Conditioning/environment | Strength changes systematically with absorbed moisture, conditioning duration, or test-room environment | Equilibrated specimens retain the original group differences |
| Test or grip artifact | Differences follow operator, frame, grip, alignment, or test order; failures cluster near grips; independent strain measurement changes conclusions | Gauge-section failures and results reproduce across qualified test setups |
| Random defect occurrence | Large within-build residual variance; strength follows weakest-defect metrics rather than nominal settings | Strength is tightly explained by stable process variables without defect mediation |

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Manufacturing provenance

For every coupon:

- printer and firmware identifiers;
- slicer version and archived build files;
- CAD and toolpath checksum;
- material manufacturer, polymer grade, color/additives, lot, spool, storage history, and age;
- drying conditions and elapsed time after drying;
- nozzle identity, nominal and measured diameter, wear history, and maintenance;
- all commanded process settings;
- plate coordinates, orientation, print sequence, and neighboring-part layout;
- operator, date, start time, build duration, pauses, alarms, and interventions;
- failed or aborted print information.

### Time-resolved process evidence

Where feasible:

- nozzle, bed, chamber, and ambient temperature;
- relative humidity;
- extrusion command, feed rate, motor current, or pressure proxy;
- fan command;
- layer and time stamps;
- power interruptions or thermal excursions.

### Coupon evidence

- raw dimensions at multiple gauge locations;
- mass and calculated bulk density;
- surface images before testing;
- internal-defect measurements on a stratified subset;
- conditioning mass before and after equilibration;
- unique identifier and complete chain of custody.

### Tensile-test evidence

- raw force, crosshead displacement, and strain time series;
- test speed, preload, grip pressure or procedure, gauge length, and alignment checks;
- calculated tensile properties;
- failure location and mode;
- images of fracture surfaces and both coupon halves;
- operator, frame, load cell, grips, software version, and test-session environment;
- all deviations, slippage, premature failures, and reasons for any invalidation.

### Sample-size evidence

Run a priori simulation-based power analysis using a plausible hierarchical variance range from pilot or historical data. Size the study to detect:

- the prespecified smallest practically important strength difference;
- meaningful variance components at printer and build levels;
- principal factor interactions;
- mediation through geometry, moisture, thermal history, or porosity.

The experimental unit for printer-level or build-level factors is the printer or build, not the number of coupons within it.

## 5. Measurement

### Primary tensile outcome

Calculate ultimate tensile strength as maximum force divided by a prespecified area:

- Primary analysis: individually measured initial gauge cross-sectional area, using a documented multi-location procedure.
- Sensitivity analysis: nominal CAD area and alternative defensible area estimates.

This comparison distinguishes true load-capacity variation from variation introduced by area normalization.

### Secondary tensile outcomes

- maximum force;
- modulus from a prespecified strain interval;
- yield stress and strain using a specified convention;
- strain at break;
- toughness or area under the stress–strain curve;
- failure location;
- valid gauge-section failure indicator;
- anisotropic or interfacial fracture classification.

### Manufacturing-factor measurement

- Verify temperatures with traceable sensors rather than relying solely on displayed setpoints.
- Measure feedstock moisture immediately before printing where practical.
- Measure extrudate or deposited-road dimensions on representative calibration structures.
- Record realized flow proxies and specimen mass.
- Quantify environmental conditions throughout printing, conditioning, and testing.

### Geometry and defects

- Measure width and thickness at several fixed gauge positions with controlled contact force or calibrated optical methods.
- Assess warpage and surface roughness.
- Estimate density using a method compatible with the polymer.
- For a stratified subset, quantify void fraction, void size, orientation, and location using nondestructive imaging or sectioned microscopy.
- Conduct fracture-surface assessment using a blinded, prespecified coding rubric.

### Quality controls

- Use traceable calibrations and daily verification checks.
- Repeat a random subset of dimensional, imaging, and classification measurements.
- Use automated extraction from raw signals where possible, with version-controlled analysis code.
- Flag observations using prespecified rules; preserve raw data and analyze exclusions in sensitivity analyses.
- Confirm that test fixtures do not systematically damage coupons or induce grip failures.

## 6. Analysis

### Descriptive and diagnostic analysis

- Plot strength, maximum force, dimensions, density, moisture, and failure mode by lot, printer, build, plate position, print order, and test session.
- Map each build spatially to identify repeatable gradients.
- Plot time trends to detect warm-up, drift, carryover, or nozzle degradation.
- Compare valid gauge failures with grip or transition failures.
- Examine distributions and heteroscedasticity without removing values solely because they are extreme.

### Variance decomposition

Fit a hierarchical mixed-effects model with appropriate random effects for:

- material lot or spool;
- printer;
- build nested within printer and day;
- plate region or spatial correlation;
- conditioning batch;
- test session;
- residual coupon variability.

Report estimated variance components and uncertainty intervals. Quantify the repeatability of printer, build, and position effects across independent replications.

### Factor-effect estimation

For the randomized multifactor phase:

- Estimate main effects, prespecified interactions, curvature, and block effects.
- Use intention-to-treat estimates based on assigned settings, supplemented by realized-setting analyses when sensor data show departures.
- Model continuous factors continuously rather than arbitrarily dichotomizing them.
- Check residuals, nonlinearities, unequal variance, and influential observations.
- Control multiplicity for secondary exploratory factors while preserving prespecified primary contrasts.
- Report effect sizes and uncertainty intervals, not only significance tests.

### Mechanism assessment

For each proposed explanation:

1. Confirm that the controlled factor changes the proposed intermediate measure.
2. Estimate whether the factor changes tensile strength.
3. Determine whether the intermediate measure predicts strength within randomized groups.
4. Assess whether adjustment for the intermediate attenuates the factor effect.
5. Verify mechanism-specific fracture or defect observations.

Mediation results will be treated cautiously because intermediates such as porosity are not themselves randomized and may be measured with error.

### Prediction versus explanation

Build two separately evaluated models:

- An explanatory model restricted to prespecified causal factors and interactions.
- A predictive model using all available process and characterization variables.

Use grouped cross-validation that withholds entire builds, days, printers, or lots—not random coupons from the same build. A factor may be predictive without being causal; causal claims require randomized contrasts or replicated natural interventions with strong controls.

### Sensitivity analyses

Repeat conclusions under:

- nominal versus measured cross-sectional area;
- all tests versus valid gauge-section failures only;
- inclusion versus exclusion of protocol deviations;
- alternative plausible hierarchical structures;
- robust error models;
- missing-data assumptions;
- separate analysis by material lot and printer;
- maximum force as an outcome to avoid area-induced artifacts.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Before data collection, define:

- the smallest practically important difference in tensile strength;
- acceptable measurement uncertainty;
- acceptable repeatability across builds;
- equivalence margins for concluding that a candidate factor is negligible within the tested range;
- minimum replication at the build—not merely coupon—level.

A candidate explanation will be considered supported only if:

1. its effect is directionally consistent and practically meaningful;
2. the effect replicates across independent builds and, where applicable, printers or lots;
3. uncertainty excludes both no effect and the prespecified negligible-effect region as appropriate;
4. the proposed intermediate observations occur;
5. major alternative explanations do not account for the result;
6. the association predicts held-out build-level data when prediction is relevant.

A factor will be considered unlikely to explain the persistent differences within the tested range if a sufficiently powered equivalence analysis places its effect inside the prespecified negligible region and no mechanism-specific evidence is observed.

Data collection may stop after the preregistered minimum sample only when:

- measurement-system acceptance criteria are met;
- every planned block and independent build replicate is complete;
- information or precision targets for the primary effects and major variance components are reached;
- no unresolved data-quality failure remains.

Optional sequential expansion may be used at prespecified checkpoints based on precision, not interim effect direction. Stop for redesign if calibration fails, randomization is compromised, environmental control leaves key factors confounded, or invalid-test rates exceed a predefined threshold.

The final conclusion may be:

- one dominant supported mechanism;
- multiple contributing mechanisms with estimated contributions;
- test-system artifact;
- unexplained residual variability;
- or insufficient evidence due to limited factor range or precision.

## 8. Limitations

- Results may be specific to the selected polymer, color/additives, printer architecture, coupon geometry, slicer, and process window.
- Standard tensile coupons may not represent the thermal history or stress state of larger components.
- Temperature, flow, and moisture sensors may not capture local bead-scale conditions.
- Internal imaging may be limited to a subset and may miss sub-resolution defects.
- Fractography and failure-mode classification can remain partly subjective despite blinding and repeatability checks.
- Some factor combinations may be unsafe or produce nonviable prints, restricting causal inference to the tested operating region.
- Machine, lot, and day effects can remain partially confounded if too few independent units are available.
- Statistical mediation cannot by itself prove a microstructural mechanism.
- Conditioning may alter coupons differently from service environments.
- Invalid or grip failures can create selection bias if excluded without sensitivity analysis.
- Even a strong predictive model may fail on new printer models, material lots, geometries, or laboratories; external validation is required before broad generalization.

