- Internal imaging may be limited to a subset and may miss sub-resolution defects.
- Fractography and failure-mode classification can remain partly subjective despite blinding and repeatability checks.
- Some factor combinations may be unsafe or produce nonviable prints, restricting causal inference to the tested operating region.
- Machine, lot, and day effects can remain partially confounded if too few independent units are available.
- Statistical mediation cannot by itself prove a microstructural mechanism.
- Conditioning may alter coupons differently from service environments.
- Invalid or grip failures can create selection bias if excluded without sensitivity analysis.
- Even a strong predictive model may fail on new printer models, material lots, geometries, or laboratories; external validation is required before broad generalization.
